          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What actually happens in the airways during an asthma flare-up?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched describe asthma only at a general level, so I can't fully explain the steps inside the airways.\n\n**What the reference says (Asthma, general clinical reference):** Asthma is a chronic inflammatory condition of the airways. The airway lining becomes inflamed, and the muscle around the airways is prone to tightening. Together these narrow the airways. The narrowing comes and goes, and it can usually be reversed, which is why symptoms change from day to day and sometimes within a single day. Symptoms are wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough, often worse at night or early in the morning, and often set off by particular triggers.\n\n**What the sources do not cover:** I could not find passages in the corpus that explain how mucus builds up during a flare-up, or what airway remodeling means in detail. I have not filled these gaps from memory. A clinician or a dedicated respiratory resource can explain them.\n\n**Safety note:** If someone during an asthma flare-up has severe breathlessness, struggles to speak, has blue or grey lips, or their reliever treatment is not helping, they need emergency help straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
